'''Distribuïdors globals de sabates de Balboa, S.A.''' (DSB), trading as '''DSB Calçat''', is a Balboan footwear import and retail company based in [[Sant Cristòfor]]. The company is the largest specialised footwear retailer in [[Balboa]], operating brand-specific shoe stores, a multi-brand retail chain, and an in-house footwear label. The company imports shoes from manufacturers across Volisania, Okaiken, Zong, Balisca, and Sinchew, and operates 86 stores across all five provinces. It is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] under the ticker DSB and is a constituent of the [[SCX 100]] index.

== History ==

DSB was founded in 1984 by Pere Orriols i Verdaguer, a former buyer for a Sant Cristòfor department store who identified that the Balboan footwear market was underserved by generalist clothing retailers. While stores such as those operated by [[Grup Wuling]] carried footwear as a secondary category, no domestic retailer specialised in shoes across multiple price points and brands.

Orriols began by importing footwear from Volisanian and Okaiken manufacturers and supplying independent shoe stores in the Sant Cristòfor area. The company's first retail store opened in 1986 in central Sant Cristòfor, stocking a range of imported brands under a single roof. The multi-brand concept proved successful, and the company expanded to Sant Bart and Costabella by 1989.

During the 1990s, DSB secured exclusive retail distribution agreements with several international footwear brands, opening brand-specific stores in Estret and Jala provinces. The company was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1997.

The establishment of the [[BTPMEU]] in 1998 reduced import costs for footwear from [[Tapuya]] and [[Potocsí]], broadening the range of brands available to the company. DSB expanded its store network into the interior provinces in the 2000s, launching the [[Passos (retail chain)|Passos]] multi-brand chain to serve lower-income markets where brand-specific stores were not viable.

== Operations ==

=== Brand-specific stores ===

DSB operates 34 brand-specific footwear stores under exclusive or near-exclusive retail distribution agreements with international brands. These stores are concentrated in [[Estret Province|Estret]] (18 stores) and [[Jala Province|Jala]] (11 stores), with the remainder in [[Nurra Province|Nurra]] and [[Migjorn Province|Migjorn]]. The brand portfolio includes:

* Volisanian footwear brands — formal and casual shoes from Volisanian manufacturers
* Okaiken footwear brands — athletic and lifestyle footwear from Okaiken
* Zong footwear brands — Fosian-style shoes and sandals
* Baliscan footwear brands — outdoor and lifestyle footwear from [[Balisca]]

Brand-specific stores are located in shopping centres and high-street retail districts in Sant Cristòfor, Sant Bart, Costabella, and Novara, targeting middle and upper-middle consumers who purchase footwear as a branded fashion item.

=== Passos ===

{{main|Passos (retail chain)}}

[[Passos (retail chain)|Passos]] is DSB's multi-brand footwear retail chain, launched in 2003. The chain operates 44 stores across all five provinces, including locations in [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] and [[Migjorn Province|Migjorn]] where brand-specific stores are not viable. Passos stores stock a rotating selection of footwear from multiple brands across price ranges, offering a self-service format with lower operating costs than the brand-specific stores.

Passos sources inventory through brand overruns, end-of-season stock, and cancelled orders, as well as first-quality current stock from brands that do not maintain standalone retail in Balboa. The chain's pricing spans from budget options at 25 to 40 hanti ($6 to $10) to mid-range branded shoes at 80 to 150 hanti ($20 to $37), making it the primary source of new footwear for lower-income consumers.

=== In-house labels ===

DSB operates two in-house footwear labels:

* '''Camí''' — a value-oriented line of basic footwear (canvas shoes, sandals, basic leather shoes), manufactured by contract producers in [[Sinchew]] and [[Tapuya]]
* '''Petjada''' — a mid-range line of casual and formal footwear, designed in Balboa and manufactured by contract producers in Sinchew and [[Balisca]]

The in-house labels account for approximately 22 per cent of total revenue and carry higher margins than the distributed brands, as DSB controls the design, production, and retail chain.

== Supply chain ==

DSB imports footwear from manufacturers across five countries, with the following approximate sourcing breakdown:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Source country !! Share of inventory !! Product types
|-
| [[Volisania]] || 28% || Formal shoes, casual leather shoes, premium footwear
|-
| [[Okaiken]] || 24% || Athletic shoes, lifestyle sneakers, technical footwear
|-
| [[Sinchew]] || 18% || Budget footwear, canvas shoes, sandals, in-house label production
|-
| [[Balisca]] || 16% || Outdoor footwear, lifestyle shoes, in-house label production
|-
| [[Kingdom of Zong|Zong]] || 9% || Fosian-style footwear, sandals, traditional shoes
|-
| [[Tapuya]] || 5% || Budget footwear, Passos chain stock
|}

Footwear is imported through the [[Portnou|Port de Portnou]] and the Sant Cristòfor logistics hub, with customs clearance handled by DSB's in-house import division. The company operates a central distribution centre in [[Sant Cristòfor]] and a secondary facility in [[Sant Bart]].

The in-house labels are produced through contract manufacturing arrangements with factories in Sinchew, Tapuya, and Balisca. DSB's design team in Sant Cristòfor develops the Camí and Petjada collections, which are manufactured to specification and shipped to Balboa for retail.

